# PUBCON    Title: Electron Impact Excitation for He-like Ions with Z = 20–42
# PUBCON  Authors: R. Si and S. Li and K. Wang and X. L. Guo and Z. B. Chen and J. Yan and C. Y. Chen and T. Brage and Y. M. Zou
# PUBCON Citation: Astron. Astrophys. 600, p. A85 (2017)
# PUBCON      DOI: https://doi.org/10.1051/0004-6361/201630027
# PUBCON  Comment: Derivation of excitation energies from the transition data reported by Si et al. 2017 involved several steps. First, this paper of Si et al. did not provide a separate table of calculated energy levels, so they had to be extracted from the listed transition wave numbers. Those were rounded too harshly in the table of Si et al. To extract more precise calculated level values, the least-squares level optimization code LOPT (Kramida 2011 ) was used here with input wave number uncertainties set to 0.33 of the unit of the last decimal figure of the listed wave number. For each nuclear charge Z in the interval 20–42, Si et al. have listed 1172 transitions between 49 energy levels. Each level had 46 to 48 transitions to other levels, so the level optimization has significantly reduced the numerical uncertainty stemming from the harsh rounding of the tabulated wave numbers. In particular, all intervals between the 1snp $^{1,3}$P°$_{0,1,2}$ and 1snl $^{S}$L$_{J}$ (n = 3–5, L = d, f, g) were derived with numerical uncertainties of a fraction of cm$^{-1}$ even for the largest Z = 42. All transition wave number data of Si et al. 2017 have demostrated excellent internal consistency. On the next step, for each 1snl $^{S}$L$_{J}$ level, the level intervals mentioned above were added to reference values of the corresponding 1snp $^{1,3}$P°$_{0,1,2}$ level (taken from Yerokhin & Surzhykov 2019 ). A straight arithmetic mean of thus obtained four values was used as an initial estimate of each 1snl $^{S}$L$_{J}$ level, while the root-mean square deviation from the mean of these four values, combined in quadrature with uncertaities of the reference $^{1,3}$P°$_{0,1,2}$ levels, provided an estimate of uncertainty. For the interval Z = 20–36, these initial estimates have been compared with similarly derived adjusted values based on the more accurate calculations of the same group (Si et al. 2016 ) made with the same many-body perturbation theory method. The energies from these two works were found to agree well with each other, with only a small systematic difference (up tp 34 cm$^{-1}$ for the levels with n ≥ 4 at Z = 36) smoothly varying as a function of Z. This systematic difference has been extrapolated to the ions with Z = 37–42, which were not covered by the work of (Si et al. 2016 ). For the 1s3d $^{1,3}$D$_{1,2,3}$ levels, we compared the initially adjusted values of Si et al. 2017 with the reference values derived in a similar way from the unified-theory calculation of Drake 1985 . The differences were also found to be small (up to 53 cm$^{-1}$ at Z = 36) and smoothly varying with Z, allowing for a confident extrapolation to Z = 37–42. The final values of the excitaion energies given here were obtained by subtracting these extrapolated systematic differences from the initial estimates described above. This adjustment was made by A. Kramida (unpublished, 2023).
